INT. ASHER GOLDBERG'S APARTMENT, KITCHEN - NIGHT

A narrow galley kitchen with stacked dishes, peeling wallpaper, a calendar from a plumbing company dated three years ago. Rain on the window.

LEONIE GOLDBERG, thirties, in her work blazer and a coat still buttoned, stands at the counter unpacking a paper bag of groceries. She sets down a loaf, a carton of milk, a box of pills, reads the label, and shelves it with the others in a row she has clearly arranged many times before.

From the bedroom down the hall comes a cough, deep and rough. Then a man's voice.

                    ASHER (O.S.)
          Leonie?

                    LEONIE
          I'm here, Papa.

                    ASHER (O.S.)
          Did you bring the soup?

                    LEONIE
          I brought the other soup.

                    ASHER (O.S.)
          Not the tomato.

                    LEONIE
          Mushroom.

                    ASHER (O.S.)
          Bah.

She turns on the stove, fills a pot, and breaks the can open with a bright little clack. She stirs. Steam rises. The cough comes again from the bedroom and this time lasts, and she stops stirring and stands with the spoon above the pot, listening to every second of it.

                    LEONIE (CONT'D)
          Papa, are you all right?

                    ASHER (O.S.)
          Fine. I'm fine. Where's your brother?

She sets the spoon on the rest, wipes her hands on a dish towel, folds it in half, and folds it again.

                    LEONIE
          Danny's in Phoenix. He said he'd call.

                    ASHER (O.S.)
          He says a lot of things.

                    LEONIE
          I know.

She walks down the hallway, past a wall of framed photographs, past the bathroom with its door open, past a pair of slippers on the carpet. She stops at the bedroom door. The light inside is dim. She raises her hand to knock, then lowers it, and instead rests her palm flat against the wood.

                    ASHER (O.S.)
          Leonie, are you standing there?

                    LEONIE
          No.

                    ASHER (O.S.)
          I can see your shadow under the door.

She laughs, brief and wet, and pushes the door open.

                    LEONIE
          Mushroom or nothing, Papa.

                    ASHER (O.S.)
          Mushroom.
